silience, reliability.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Concerns: relatively weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. So strengths: reliability (conscientious), stress resilience, technical (weather, computer). Concerns: crew cooperation (nasty remarks), attention (weak memory for numbers). Could be problematic.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education. Strengths: decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence. Concerns: crew cooperation, adaptability, communication.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concerns: regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper. Strengths: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, responsibility. Concerns: crew cooperation (know-it-all, hot temper). \n\nAll have some concerns. Which is strongest overall? Need to weigh. Candidate B has strong stress handling and conscientiousness but nasty remarks and weak memory for numbers (attention). Candidate C has good decision making and calm, but communication issues and egocentric. Candidate D has strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper and know-it-all may affect crew cooperation. Candidate A has technical vision and leadership but show-off and not open to new ideas.\n\nWhich candidate best balances? Possibly Candidate D: strong reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, responsibility, concentration. The hot temper and know-it-all are concerns but maybe less severe than egocentric or nasty remarks? Hard to decide. Could also be Candidate B: conscientious and stress handling, but nasty remarks could be severe for crew cooperation. I'd lean D.\n\nWe need to state recommendation and give evidence. Since no prior discussion, we can just state our recommendation and give evidence. Provide at least one fact per candidate? Not required but we need to give evidence for our recommendation. Provide strengths and concerns.\n\nLet's produce public message: \"I recommend Candidate D because...\" Provide evidence: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concern: hot temper, know-it-all. Explain why strengths outweigh concerns relative to others.\n\nOk."}
